---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Lightbox from "../components/Lightbox.astro";

/* Each section pairs a claim with the screenshot that evidences it. The
 * dashboard capture is deliberately absent: it currently photographs an ingest
 * reading "Offline" while audio is demonstrably flowing, which is a defect in
 * the capture harness and the product's state reporting, not something to put
 * on a marketing page.
 *
 * `note` is the EXPERIMENTAL convention from the product, carried onto the
 * site. It exists because this page — not a Go doc comment, not a card in the
 * routing editor — is where most people meet a feature for the first time, and
 * a caveat that appears only after install is a caveat that arrived too late.
 * Same rule as ui/src/components/Experimental.tsx: it NAMES the specific claim
 * without evidence rather than saying "beta", and it labels rather than gates —
 * nothing here is turned off, and the surrounding copy stays a description of
 * what the feature does. */

const sections = [
  {
    id: "routing",
    eyebrow: "Audio routing",
    title: "Choose the tracks, per destination",
    body: [
      "The clean feed to one platform, the full mix to the next, from the same ingest. Checkbox-per-track in simple mode, or a full channel-to-output mix matrix with a gain per cell when you need one input channel in one output channel at a specific level.",
      "Nothing is filtered until a destination asks for it. Track annotations describe the ingest — what track 3 actually is — and save on their own, independent of any destination.",
    ],
    shot: "02-routing.png",
    caption: "Three destinations, three different track selections — and the switch that keeps a music bed out of one of them.",
    alt: "polyemesis routing page: per-destination track selection with music policy and loudness target",
  },
  {
    id: "vod",
    eyebrow: "Two mixes, one connection",
    title: "The broadcast keeps the music. The archive does not.",
    body: [
      "Twitch Enhanced Broadcasting negotiates a second audio track alongside the live one. polyemesis builds both from the same ingest, in one filter graph, and sends them over one connection — the live mix with the music bed, and a VOD track without it. No second upload, no second encode of the picture.",
      "A single video rendition is enough. Multitrack video is not a precondition for the second audio track, which is the part most people expect to be required — the feature is named for it and does not need it.",
      "It needs a GPU, and it needs you to say so. Twitch refuses the negotiation outright if the request reports no GPU, so this is the one thing here a headless server cannot use. polyemesis does not guess at your hardware — you declare it in Settings, because Twitch validates the inventory it is sent rather than the card doing the encoding, and a fabricated one is refused by name. Leave it blank and the destination publishes to the ordinary Twitch ingest and tells you once, which is the right behaviour on the majority of installs rather than an error.",
      "Every other destination still receives its own single mix, exactly as before. The picture is still copied rather than re-encoded; the second track is a second AAC encode, which is cheap and is not free.",
    ],
    note: "The negotiation itself is proven: polyemesis's tests reach ingest.twitch.tv on every run, and Twitch accepts a supported-GPU inventory, grants the VOD audio track and mints a key. What nobody has watched yet is a broadcast published through that key — the last step, and the one this feature is for. Nothing is gated on that: a negotiation that does not succeed falls back to the ordinary Twitch ingest, which is what every install used before this existed.",
    shot: "02-routing.png",
    caption: "The archive mix is per destination, and off unless asked for.",
    alt: "polyemesis routing page: per-destination track selection with music policy and loudness target",
  },
  {
    id: "matrix",
    eyebrow: "Mix matrix",
    title: "Channel-level control when the checkboxes are not enough",
    body: [
      "A grid of input channels against output channels, each cell a gain. This is what a 5.1 downmix, a mid-side fold, or a mono guest feed into one side needs.",
      "The resolved FFmpeg filtergraph is shown before you apply it, so nothing about the mix is hidden behind the UI.",
    ],
    shot: "08-mix-matrix.png",
    caption: "Input channels down, output channels across, a gain in every cell. The resolved filtergraph is shown before it is applied.",
    alt: "polyemesis mix matrix: input channels against output channels with a gain in each cell",
  },
  {
    id: "renditions",
    eyebrow: "Renditions",
    title: "One shared encode feeds every destination that selects it",
    body: [
      "For the platforms that will not take your source — a 4K60 ingest that Twitch or Kick refuses. One encode, ref-counted, so an unused tier costs nothing.",
      "It re-encodes video only. Audio still routes per destination underneath it, which is the whole point.",
    ],
    note: "Hardware encoding is offered on NVENC, QSV, VA-API, AMF and VideoToolbox, and polyemesis test-encodes a frame with each one at startup so an encoder that is listed but unusable is caught before a broadcast is. The command-line flags it hands the NVENC, QSV, VA-API and AMF encoders — rate control, preset, profile — are the part still without evidence: they were read out of FFmpeg's own option tables rather than measured on silicon. VideoToolbox is not in that set; a real encode with its own flags is run per build.",
    shot: "06-renditions.png",
    caption: "One shared encode, ref-counted across the destinations that selected it. Audio still routes per destination underneath.",
    alt: "polyemesis renditions page: shared video encodes with overlays and hardware encoder selection",
  },
  {
    id: "monitoring",
    eyebrow: "Monitoring",
    title: "Every process, with its own FFmpeg output",
    body: [
      "Host CPU, memory, relay throughput, ingest loss, and the live stderr of each child process — the thing you actually need at 3am when one destination is unhappy and the others are fine.",
      "Prometheus metrics and in-process alert rules with webhook delivery, so a stream that stops can page you rather than wait to be noticed.",
    ],
    shot: "07-monitoring.png",
    caption: "Each child process with its own live FFmpeg stderr, beside host CPU, memory and ingest loss.",
    alt: "polyemesis monitoring page: process table, host resources and live FFmpeg log",
  },
  {
    id: "recording",
    eyebrow: "Recording and post",
    title: "The full multitrack archive, stream-copied",
    body: [
      "Segmented recording that preserves every one of the split audio tracks your encoder sent, with retention by age, total size, and a free-space floor that accounts for files polyemesis did not write.",
      "That archive is where your own VOD track comes from. Every track is kept unmixed, so the upload you cut afterwards need not carry the music that went out live.",
      "Then a job queue that yields to the live stream: Whisper transcription with full-text search, proxies, thumbnails, and a clipper. A dropped frame is unrecoverable; a transcript arriving an hour later costs only the wait.",
    ],
    shot: "15-recordings.png",
    caption: "Segments with every ingest track preserved, next to retention by age and size and the free-space floor.",
    alt: "polyemesis recordings page: segment list with tracks, stems, retention settings and disk usage",
  },
  {
    id: "chat",
    eyebrow: "Chat",
    title: "Five platforms in one timeline",
    body: [
      "One merged feed reading YouTube, Twitch, Kick, Facebook and Rumble. The send box fans out to YouTube, Twitch and Kick, with per-platform verdicts when half the fan-out fails, because partial delivery is the normal case. Facebook is read and moderate only; Rumble is read only, and polls, because its API offers no socket.",
      "Delete everywhere, hide on Facebook, ban and timeout on YouTube, Twitch and Kick — and a moderator card showing what one person has said across every platform at once.",
    ],
    shot: "09-chat.png",
    caption: "One timeline across five platforms, with a per-platform verdict when only half a fan-out succeeds.",
    alt: "polyemesis chat page: merged timeline across platforms with a single send box",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Multistreaming features — a different audio mix per platform"
  description="Route your OBS split audio tracks per destination: a channel-level mix matrix, shared renditions and multitrack recording. Self-hosted."
  path="/features"
>
  <section class="wrap py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Features</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      A broadcast server built around one feature.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      The routing is the reason it exists. Everything below is what a server has
      to do as well, if the routing is going to be usable in a real broadcast.
    </p>
    <nav class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2" aria-label="Sections">
      {
        sections.map((s) => (
          <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="chip transition-colors hover:border-primary hover:text-fg">
            {s.eyebrow}
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </nav>
  </section>

  {
    sections.map((s, i) => (
      <section
        id={s.id}
        class:list={["border-t border-line py-16 sm:py-20", i % 2 === 1 && "bg-surface/30"]}
      >
        {/* THE SIDE ALTERNATES, because six of these in a row did not.

            Measured before this: #routing #matrix #renditions #monitoring
            #recording and #chat were 593px tall EACH -- six identical blocks,
            zero variance, every one a 510px text column beside a 690px figure
            in the same order. That is a conveyor belt, and it tells a reader
            that all six capabilities weigh the same, which is the opposite of
            what this page is arguing.

            The ratio swaps WITH the order so the screenshot keeps the wider
            track either way; the figure is what a reader is here to look at,
            and shrinking it on alternate rows to get variety would be paying
            for rhythm with legibility.

            DOM order is unchanged -- heading, prose, then figure -- so reading
            order and tab order still match the argument. Only the visual
            columns swap, via `order`, which is what `order` is for. */}
        <div
          class:list={[
            "wrap grid gap-10 lg:items-start",
            i % 2 === 0 ? "lg:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr]" : "lg:grid-cols-[1.15fr_0.85fr]",
          ]}
        >
          <div class:list={["lg:sticky lg:top-20", i % 2 === 1 && "lg:order-last"]}>
            <p class="eyebrow">{s.eyebrow}</p>
            <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">{s.title}</h2>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
              {s.body.map((p) => (
                <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">{p}</p>
              ))}
            </div>
            {/* Neutral border, not a signal colour, for the reason the product
                gives: "unverified" is a property of the feature, not a state
                anything is in. It sits AFTER the description so the feature is
                described before it is qualified — a caveat first reads as a
                warning not to bother. */}
            {s.note && (
              <div class="mt-5 rounded-token border border-line bg-surface/60 p-4">
                <span class="chip">experimental</span>
                <p class="mt-2 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">{s.note}</p>
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
          <figure data-reveal>
            {/* The button wraps the image rather than sitting beside it, so the
                whole screenshot is the target -- a 12px magnifier icon on a
                2880-wide image would be a worse hit area than the image itself.
                Styled to look like nothing, so the page is unchanged until
                clicked. */}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="shot-open"
              data-shot={`/shots/${s.shot}`}
              data-shot-alt={s.alt}
              aria-label={`Expand screenshot: ${s.alt}`}
            >
              <img
                src={`/shots/${s.shot}`}
                alt={s.alt}
                width="2880"
                height="1800"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                class="w-full rounded-token border border-line"
                data-shot-img
              />
            </button>
            {/* A CAPTION SAYS WHAT TO NOTICE. None of the six had one, while
                the single figure on the home page did.

                Deliberately NOT a restatement of `alt`: both are exposed to
                assistive technology, so duplicating one in the other makes a
                screen reader read the same sentence twice. `alt` describes the
                picture for someone who cannot see it; this points a reader who
                CAN see it at the part that matters. */}
            <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
              {s.caption}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------- who it is for -- */}
  {/* PROSE, NOT PAGES, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE CHOICE BACKED BY DATA.

      Each of these three is a real workflow the audio routing already supports,
      and each was checked for search demand before being written (DataForSEO,
      16 August 2026). All three returned NOTHING above 20 searches a month:
      multi-language and interpretation streaming, live stream transcription,
      captions and accessibility, corporate and conference streaming. The
      "church live streaming" cluster looks bigger at 22,430/month and is worse
      -- it is almost entirely people looking for one named church's own Sunday
      service, not for software.

      So there is no SEO case for a page each, and building four would be four
      thin pages chasing an audience that is not searching those words. There IS
      a case for saying them out loud HERE, where a reader who already arrived is
      deciding whether this product is for them. These earn their place by
      recognition, not by traffic, and this comment exists so nobody later
      "promotes" them to pages on the assumption that nobody checked. */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16 sm:py-20" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Who it is for</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-3xl font-medium">
        Three rooms where one mix is the thing that does not work.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium">Two languages, one camera</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The floor mic on track 1, the interpreter on track 2. The English
            channel gets one, the Spanish channel gets the other, and both get
            the same video from the same upload. Adding a third language is a
            checkbox and another destination, not another encoder.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium">The archive that differs from the broadcast</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The live broadcast carries the music bed. A second mix, built from
            the same ingest, leaves it out — for a recording, a file
            destination, or Twitch's VOD track. One upload, one video encode.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
            The Twitch route needs a GPU, and is Twitch-only.
            <a href="/twitch-vod-track" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">What that involves →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium">Vertical and horizontal, at once</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            A 16:9 composition and a 9:16 one are two programmes, not one
            cropped from the other — which is exactly the case sources were
            built for. Run both from one install, each with its own ingest,
            destinations and mixes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------- how it compares ---- */}
  {/* On the features page on purpose: a reader deciding between these three is
      doing it while reading the capability list, not after. Every row is a
      thing polyemesis does that the other two do not — checkable against your
      own install rather than asserted. */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16 sm:py-20" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it compares</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-3xl font-medium">
        What only polyemesis does.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Against the two servers people weigh it against: datarhei Restreamer,
        the self-hosted standard, and restream.io, the hosted one. The OBS
        plugins most readers already have — obs-multi-rtmp, Aitum Multistream —
        and MistServer are on the full comparison, where the distinction is that
        they select an audio track per destination rather than mix one.
      </p>

      {/* THE FULL TABLE USED TO BE REPEATED HERE, all eight rows of it, and it
          was character-for-character identical to the primary table on
          /comparison -- 9 of 9 rows, verified.

          It cost authority rather than bytes. A reader who studied it here and
          met the same table again on /comparison learned that the page named
          for the comparison had nothing further to offer, which is the wrong
          thing to teach about the page doing the arguing. Keeping two copies
          honest also needed a build check, and that check existed because they
          had already drifted twice.

          What is left is the one row that is actually the product's thesis, and
          a door to the page that carries the evidence. The claim still appears
          on this page; the argument now lives in exactly one place. */}
      <div class="mt-8 rounded-token border border-line bg-ink/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">
          The row that matters
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-balance text-lg leading-snug text-fg">
          Per-destination audio mix from one multitrack ingest.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          polyemesis <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-live">Yes</span> ·
          Restreamer <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-subtle">No</span> ·
          restream.io <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-subtle">No</span>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          All three restream one input to many platforms. The difference is what
          happens to the audio on the way: polyemesis is the only one that lets
          <em>this</em> platform take the clean mix while <em>that</em> one takes
          the full mix, from a single upload and without re-encoding the picture.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5">
          <a href="/comparison" class="inline-block py-1 text-sm text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">
            All eight capabilities, against five products →
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap py-20 text-center">
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">See it running in two minutes.</h2>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install</a>
      <a href="/comparison" class="btn btn-ghost">How it compares</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Last in the page, once, for every screenshot above. */}
  <Lightbox />
</Base>
